# Proud history, part 1: Arthur, Owain Glyndŵr's letters, Iolo Morganwg, Welsh and Latin.
# House rules for this site's voice (the owner's): proud of Wales and its people; nothing that
# runs down the figures of Welsh history; Arthur is a king, never "myth" or "legend"; contested
# points are given in the tradition's own words ("Welsh tradition holds", "Owain wrote").
# Kings are called kings (brenin), never chieftain, war leader or prince: see "Kings are kings" in EDITORIAL.md.
# features.py, long.py and data.py pull these in at their ends.

ARTHUR = dict(id="arthur", era="saints", art="sword", mins=6, people=["arthur"],
  links=[("person-arthur.html", "Arthur: his line, his battles and a map of Arthur’s Wales", "Arthur: ei linach, ei frwydrau a map o Gymru Arthur"),
         ("story-wilson-blackett.html", "Alan Wilson and Baram Blackett, the men who went looking for Arthur", "Alan Wilson a Baram Blackett, y dynion a aeth i chwilio am Arthur")],
  en="Arthur, King of the Britons", cy="Arthur, Brenin y Brythoniaid",
  sumEn="Welsh tradition holds that Arthur was a king of Gwent and Glywysing: Athrwys ap Meurig, grandson of the warrior saint Tewdrig.",
  sumCy="Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, brenin Gwent a Glywysing oedd Arthur: Athrwys ap Meurig, ŵyr y sant a’r rhyfelwr Tewdrig.",
  pEn=["Before he belonged to the world, Arthur belonged to Wales. Every one of the earliest writings about him is Welsh, set down in Welsh or by Welshmen in Latin, centuries before the romances of France and England. Welsh tradition holds that he was a king of the south-east: Athrwys ap Meurig, king of Gwent and Glywysing.",
   "## The king in the charters",
   "Athrwys is named in the charters of the Book of Llandaff and in the old royal pedigrees. He was the son of King Meurig ap Tewdrig and of Onbrawst, daughter of Gwrgan Fawr of Ergyng, and the father of Morgan, the king whose name is held to live on in Morgannwg, Glamorgan. His dates are usually given as about 605 to 655.",
   "His grandfather Tewdrig had given up the crown to live as a hermit at Tintern. When the Saxons crossed into Gwent the old king took up his sword once more, drove them back at the ford on the Wye, and died of his wounds at Mathern, where his church still stands. He is honoured as a saint and a martyr. Meurig reigned after him, and Athrwys grew up in a house that had already beaten the invader.",
   "## Why Gwent",
   "The oldest accounts set Arthur in exactly this country. Geoffrey of Monmouth, a man of Gwent himself, placed Arthur’s court at Caerleon on the Usk, in the heart of the kingdom of Meurig and Athrwys. The Lives of the saints of Glamorgan and Gwent, Cadog and Illtud among them, bring Arthur to their doors; Illtud is called his cousin. In Culhwch ac Olwen, the oldest Arthurian tale in any language, he hunts the great boar Twrch Trwyth across south Wales and into the Severn. And the name itself is close: Athrwys, Arthwys, Arthur.",
   "The case was set out in print in the eighteenth century, when the historians of Monmouthshire and Glamorgan went back to the Llandaff charters, and it has been held and argued for in Wales ever since. In our own time the Cardiff researchers Alan Wilson and Baram Blackett carried the case further in their books. Other historians may see it differently.",
   "## The earliest witnesses",
   "A line in Y Gododdin praises a warrior who fed the ravens on the rampart, ‘though he was no Arthur’. The Historia Brittonum of about 829 lists his twelve victories, ending at Mount Badon. The Annales Cambriae record Badon, and the battle of Camlan ‘in which Arthur and Medrawd fell’. The Black Book of Carmarthen says of him: ‘anoeth bid bet y Arthur’, a wonder of the world is the grave of Arthur. No one knows where he lies.",
   "## The king who sleeps"],
  pCy=["Cyn iddo berthyn i’r byd, i Gymru y perthynai Arthur. Cymreig yw pob un o’r ysgrifau cynharaf amdano, wedi’u llunio yn Gymraeg neu gan Gymry yn Lladin, ganrifoedd cyn rhamantau Ffrainc a Lloegr. Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, brenin o’r de-ddwyrain ydoedd: Athrwys ap Meurig, brenin Gwent a Glywysing.",
   "## Y brenin yn y siarteri",
   "Enwir Athrwys yn siarteri Llyfr Llandaf ac yn yr hen achau brenhinol. Mab ydoedd i’r Brenin Meurig ap Tewdrig ac Onbrawst, merch Gwrgan Fawr o Ergyng, a thad Morgan, y brenin y cedwir ei enw, yn ôl y traddodiad, ym Morgannwg. Rhoddir ei ddyddiadau fel arfer fel tua 605 hyd 655.",
   "Roedd ei daid Tewdrig wedi ildio’r goron i fyw fel meudwy yn Nhyndyrn. Pan groesodd y Saeson i Went cododd yr hen frenin ei gleddyf unwaith eto, eu gyrru’n ôl wrth y rhyd ar afon Gwy, a marw o’i glwyfau ym Merthyr Tewdrig, lle saif ei eglwys o hyd. Fe’i hanrhydeddir yn sant ac yn ferthyr. Teyrnasodd Meurig ar ei ôl, a magwyd Athrwys ar aelwyd a oedd eisoes wedi trechu’r goresgynnwr.",
   "## Pam Gwent",
   "Yn yr union wlad hon y mae’r adroddiadau hynaf yn gosod Arthur. Gosododd Sieffre o Fynwy, gŵr o Went ei hun, lys Arthur yng Nghaerllion ar Wysg, yng nghanol teyrnas Meurig ac Athrwys. Mae Bucheddau saint Morgannwg a Gwent, Cadog ac Illtud yn eu plith, yn dod ag Arthur at eu drysau; gelwir Illtud yn gefnder iddo. Yn Culhwch ac Olwen, y stori Arthuraidd hynaf mewn unrhyw iaith, mae’n hela’r Twrch Trwyth ar draws y de ac i afon Hafren. Ac mae’r enw ei hun yn agos: Athrwys, Arthwys, Arthur.",
   "Rhoddwyd yr achos mewn print yn y ddeunawfed ganrif, pan aeth haneswyr Mynwy a Morgannwg yn ôl at siarteri Llandaf, ac mae wedi’i arddel a’i ddadlau yng Nghymru byth er hynny. Yn ein hoes ni aeth yr ymchwilwyr o Gaerdydd, Alan Wilson a Baram Blackett, â’r achos ymhellach yn eu llyfrau. Gall haneswyr eraill weld pethau’n wahanol.",
   "## Y tystion cynharaf",
   "Mae llinell yn Y Gododdin yn canmol rhyfelwr a borthodd y brain ar fur y gaer, ‘er nad Arthur ydoedd’. Mae’r Historia Brittonum, tua 829, yn rhestru ei ddeuddeg buddugoliaeth, gan orffen ym Mynydd Baddon. Mae’r Annales Cambriae yn cofnodi Baddon, a brwydr Camlan ‘lle syrthiodd Arthur a Medrawd’. Dywed Llyfr Du Caerfyrddin amdano: ‘anoeth bid bet y Arthur’, rhyfeddod byd yw bedd Arthur. Ni ŵyr neb ble mae’n gorwedd.",
   "## Y brenin sy’n cysgu"],
  endEn=["In the 1130s Geoffrey of Monmouth made him the most famous king in Europe, and France later added the Round Table and the Grail. But the king himself is ours: a king of the Britons, of Gwent and Glywysing, whose people are still here and still speak his language."],
  endCy=["Yn y 1130au gwnaeth Sieffre o Fynwy ef yn frenin enwocaf Ewrop, ac ychwanegodd Ffrainc y Ford Gron a’r Greal yn ddiweddarach. Ond ein brenin ni yw ef: brenin y Brythoniaid, brenin Gwent a Glywysing, y mae ei bobl yma o hyd ac yn dal i siarad ei iaith."])

GLYNDWR = dict(id="glyndwr", era="medieval", art="torch", mins=8,
  en="The letters of Owain Glyndŵr", cy="Llythyrau Owain Glyndŵr",
  sumEn="He wrote to the kings of Scotland and France and the lords of Ireland as their equal, and set out on parchment a free Wales with its own parliament, church and universities.",
  sumCy="Ysgrifennodd at frenhinoedd yr Alban a Ffrainc ac arglwyddi Iwerddon fel un cydradd â hwy, a rhoi ar femrwn Gymru rydd â’i senedd, ei heglwys a’i phrifysgolion ei hun.",
  pEn=["On 16 September 1400, at Glyndyfrdwy on the Dee, Owain ap Gruffudd Fychan was proclaimed Prince of Wales. He was about forty, trained in the law and proven in war, descended through his father from the princes of Powys and through his mother from the house of Deheubarth, and the poets had long sung of him as the heir of them all. Within four years he held almost the whole country. What sets him apart from every other leader of his age is what he did with a pen. His letters show a statesman building a nation.",
   "## To the King of Scotland, 1401",
   "In 1401 Owain wrote, in French, to Robert III, King of Scots. He began with the history the two nations shared. Brutus, he wrote, ‘your most noble ancestor and mine’, was the first crowned king who dwelt in this realm, which of old was called Great Britain. Brutus had three sons: Albanactus, Locrinus and Camber. From Albanactus the King of Scots was descended in direct line. The line of Camber reigned royally down to Cadwaladr, the last crowned king of the Britons, ‘from whom I, your simple cousin, am descended in direct line’.",
   "Since Cadwaladr’s day, Owain went on, he and his people had lived under ‘the tyranny and bondage of mine and your mortal foes, the Saxons’. The prophecy said that he would be delivered from them by the king’s aid, and he asked for men-at-arms, promising to serve the king all the days of his life. It is a letter from one royal house to another: a prince and a king of the oldest blood in Britain, cousins by their descent from Brutus.",
   "## To the lords of Ireland",
   "With it went letters in Latin to the lords of Ireland. The prophecy, Owain told them, said that before the Welsh could have the upper hand, ‘you and yours, our well-beloved cousins in Ireland, must stretch forth hereto a helping hand’. He reminded them that for as long as Wales fought on, Ireland would have peace. Both letters survive because Adam of Usk, a Welsh lawyer and chronicler, copied them word for word into his chronicle.",
   "## The alliance with France, 1404",
   "By 1404 Owain had taken the great castles of Harlech and Aberystwyth, been crowned, and called a parliament of all Wales to Machynlleth. On 10 May, from Dolgellau, he issued letters in the style of a sovereign: ‘Owain, by the grace of God, Prince of Wales’, dated ‘in the fourth year of our principate’. They named his chancellor, Gruffudd Young, and his brother-in-law, John Hanmer, as ambassadors to Charles VI of France. On 14 July in Paris the two men sealed a treaty of alliance between Wales and France. The next summer a French army landed at Milford Haven and marched with Owain as far as Woodbury Hill, near Worcester.",
   "His great seal shows him enthroned with crown and sceptre, and on the other side riding to war under the four lions of Gwynedd. It is the seal of a king.",
   "## The Tripartite Indenture, 1405",
   "In February 1405 Owain sealed an agreement with Edmund Mortimer and Henry Percy, Earl of Northumberland, to divide the whole realm between them. Percy was to have the north of England and Mortimer the south. Owain’s share was a greater Wales. Its border was to run along the Severn to the north gate of Worcester, from there to the ash trees of Onennau Meigion on the road from Bridgnorth to Kinver, north by the old road to the source of the Trent, on to the source of the Mersey, and down that river to the sea. Much of what is now Cheshire, Shropshire and Herefordshire, old British country, would have come home to Wales.",
   "## The Pennal Letter, 1406",
   "The greatest of the letters was written at Pennal, near Machynlleth, on 31 March 1406, after Owain had taken counsel with the nobles and clergy of Wales. Addressed to Charles VI, it agreed to recognise the Pope at Avignon, as France did, and set out what a free Wales would be. St Davids was to be restored as an archbishopric, the head of a Welsh church that owed nothing to Canterbury. Its clergy were to be men who knew Welsh. The wealth of the Welsh church was to stay in Wales. And Wales was to have two universities, one in the north and one in the south.",
   "No other ruler in these islands had set down such a programme. The University of Wales received its charter in 1893, the Church in Wales became independent of Canterbury in 1920, and a Welsh parliament met again in 1999. Each of them is in the Pennal Letter. The letter itself, in Latin on parchment with Owain’s seal, is kept in the national archives of France in Paris. It came home on loan to the National Library in Aberystwyth in 2000, and facsimiles were presented to Welsh institutions in 2009.",
   "## Never taken",
   "Owain was never captured and never betrayed, though there was a price on his head and his own people knew the hills where he was. In 1415 the new king, Henry V, offered him a pardon. He did not answer. He is last heard of in 1412 and is thought to have died about 1415. No one has ever found his grave, and the bards said he would come again.",
   "Six centuries later his banner flies across Wales every 16 September, Owain Glyndŵr Day. A parliament sits in Wales, and the universities he planned are full. The Wales he described in his letters is the Wales we are still building."],
  pCy=["Ar 16 Medi 1400, yng Nglyndyfrdwy ar lan afon Dyfrdwy, cyhoeddwyd Owain ap Gruffudd Fychan yn Dywysog Cymru. Roedd tua deugain oed, wedi’i hyfforddi yn y gyfraith ac wedi’i brofi mewn rhyfel, yn disgyn drwy ei dad o dywysogion Powys a thrwy ei fam o dŷ Deheubarth, ac roedd y beirdd ers tro wedi canu iddo fel etifedd y cyfan. O fewn pedair blynedd roedd bron y wlad i gyd yn ei ddwylo. Yr hyn sy’n ei osod ar wahân i bob arweinydd arall yn ei oes yw’r hyn a wnaeth â’i ysgrifbin. Mae ei lythyrau’n dangos gwladweinydd yn adeiladu cenedl.",
   "## At Frenin yr Alban, 1401",
   "Yn 1401 ysgrifennodd Owain, yn Ffrangeg, at Robert III, Brenin yr Albanwyr. Dechreuodd â’r hanes a rannai’r ddwy genedl. Brutus, meddai, ‘eich hynafiad tra bonheddig chwi a minnau’, oedd y brenin coronog cyntaf a drigodd yn y deyrnas hon, a elwid gynt yn Brydain Fawr. Roedd gan Brutus dri mab: Albanactus, Locrinus a Camber. O Albanactus y disgynnai Brenin yr Albanwyr mewn llinach union. Teyrnasodd llinach Camber yn frenhinol hyd at Gadwaladr, brenin coronog olaf y Brythoniaid, ‘yr wyf fi, eich cefnder distadl, yn disgyn ohono mewn llinach union’.",
   "Er dyddiau Cadwaladr, meddai Owain, roedd ef a’i bobl wedi byw dan ‘ormes a chaethiwed fy ngelynion marwol i a chwithau, y Saeson’. Dywedai’r broffwydoliaeth y câi ei waredu rhagddynt drwy gymorth y brenin, a gofynnodd am wŷr arfog, gan addo gwasanaethu’r brenin holl ddyddiau ei oes. Llythyr o un tŷ brenhinol at un arall ydyw: tywysog a brenin o waed hynaf Prydain, yn gefndryd drwy eu disgyniad o Brutus.",
   "## At arglwyddi Iwerddon",
   "Gydag ef aeth llythyrau Lladin at arglwyddi Iwerddon. Dywedai’r broffwydoliaeth, meddai Owain wrthynt, fod yn rhaid, cyn y gallai’r Cymry gael y llaw uchaf, ‘i chwi a’r eiddoch, ein cefndryd annwyl yn Iwerddon, estyn llaw i’n cynorthwyo’. Atgoffodd hwy y câi Iwerddon heddwch cyhyd ag y daliai Cymru i ymladd. Mae’r ddau lythyr wedi goroesi am i Adda o Frynbuga, cyfreithiwr a chroniclydd o Gymro, eu copïo air am air i’w gronicl.",
   "## Y cynghrair â Ffrainc, 1404",
   "Erbyn 1404 roedd Owain wedi cipio cestyll mawr Harlech ac Aberystwyth, wedi’i goroni, ac wedi galw senedd o Gymru gyfan i Fachynlleth. Ar 10 Mai, o Ddolgellau, cyhoeddodd lythyrau yn null penadur: ‘Owain, drwy ras Duw, Tywysog Cymru’, wedi’u dyddio ‘ym mhedwaredd flwyddyn ein tywysogaeth’. Enwent ei ganghellor, Gruffudd Young, a’i frawd yng nghyfraith, John Hanmer, yn llysgenhadon at Siarl VI o Ffrainc. Ar 14 Gorffennaf ym Mharis seliodd y ddau gytundeb cynghrair rhwng Cymru a Ffrainc. Yr haf canlynol glaniodd byddin Ffrengig yn Aberdaugleddau a gorymdeithio gydag Owain cyn belled â Woodbury Hill, ger Caerwrangon.",
   "Mae ei sêl fawr yn ei ddangos ar ei orsedd â choron a theyrnwialen, ac ar yr ochr arall yn marchogaeth i ryfel dan bedwar llew Gwynedd. Sêl brenin ydyw.",
   "## Y Cytundeb Tridarn, 1405",
   "Ym mis Chwefror 1405 seliodd Owain gytundeb ag Edmund Mortimer a Henry Percy, Iarll Northumberland, i rannu’r deyrnas gyfan rhyngddynt. Percy a gâi ogledd Lloegr a Mortimer y de. Cymru fwy oedd rhan Owain. Rhedai ei ffin ar hyd afon Hafren hyd at borth gogleddol Caerwrangon, oddi yno at yr ynn yn Onennau Meigion ar y ffordd o Bridgnorth i Kinver, tua’r gogledd ar hyd yr hen ffordd at darddiad afon Trent, ymlaen at darddiad afon Mersi, ac i lawr yr afon honno i’r môr. Byddai llawer o’r hyn sydd heddiw’n swyddi Caer, Amwythig a Henffordd, hen dir y Brythoniaid, wedi dod adref i Gymru.",
   "## Llythyr Pennal, 1406",
   "Ym Mhennal, ger Machynlleth, ar 31 Mawrth 1406 yr ysgrifennwyd y mwyaf o’r llythyrau, wedi i Owain ymgynghori ag uchelwyr a chlerigwyr Cymru. Wedi’i gyfeirio at Siarl VI, cytunai i gydnabod y Pab yn Avignon, fel y gwnâi Ffrainc, a gosodai allan beth fyddai Cymru rydd. Adferid Tyddewi’n archesgobaeth, yn ben ar eglwys Gymreig heb fod yn atebol i Gaergaint. Gwŷr a fedrai’r Gymraeg fyddai ei chlerigwyr. Arhosai cyfoeth eglwys Cymru yng Nghymru. A châi Cymru ddwy brifysgol, un yn y gogledd ac un yn y de.",
   "Nid oedd yr un rheolwr arall yn yr ynysoedd hyn wedi rhoi’r fath raglen ar glawr. Cafodd Prifysgol Cymru ei siarter yn 1893, daeth yr Eglwys yng Nghymru’n annibynnol ar Gaergaint yn 1920, a chyfarfu senedd Gymreig eto yn 1999. Mae pob un ohonynt yn Llythyr Pennal. Cedwir y llythyr ei hun, yn Lladin ar femrwn â sêl Owain, yn archifau cenedlaethol Ffrainc ym Mharis. Daeth adref ar fenthyg i’r Llyfrgell Genedlaethol yn Aberystwyth yn 2000, a chyflwynwyd copïau union i sefydliadau Cymru yn 2009.",
   "## Heb ei ddal erioed",
   "Ni ddaliwyd Owain erioed ac ni chafodd ei fradychu, er bod pris ar ei ben a’i bobl ei hun yn gwybod ym mha fryniau yr oedd. Yn 1415 cynigiodd y brenin newydd, Harri V, bardwn iddo. Nid atebodd. Yn 1412 y clywir amdano ddiwethaf, a thybir iddo farw tua 1415. Ni chafwyd hyd i’w fedd erioed, a dywedai’r beirdd y deuai eto.",
   "Chwe chanrif yn ddiweddarach mae ei faner yn chwifio ledled Cymru bob 16 Medi, Dydd Owain Glyndŵr. Mae senedd yn eistedd yng Nghymru, ac mae’r prifysgolion a gynlluniodd yn llawn. Y Gymru a ddisgrifiodd yn ei lythyrau yw’r Gymru yr ydym yn dal i’w hadeiladu."])

# Welsh and Latin: goes into the language long read after its first paragraph.
LATIN_EN = ["## A sister to Latin",
   "Welsh may be closer kin to Latin than to any other great language of Europe. Many scholars hold that the tongue of the Britons and the tongues of Italy were once a single speech before they parted: one branch became Latin, the other became Welsh. On this view Cymraeg and Latin are sisters.",
   "The evidence is in the grammar, where languages change most slowly. Latin says ‘most’ with -issimus; Welsh says it with -af, older -haf, and both come from one ancient ending, -isamos. Latin has a passive in -r, amatur, ‘is loved’; so has Welsh, cerir. Both once marked ‘of’ on a noun with the same long -i, and both built the subjunctive in the same way. Both also changed the old word for five, penkwe, in the same unusual manner into kwenkwe, which gave Latin quinque and Welsh pump.",
   "Then there are the words that are cousins from birth, not borrowed: tri and tres, cant and centum, môr and mare, tir and terra, rhi and rex, gwir and verus, hen and senex, enw and nomen, ci and canis, tarw and taurus. Some scholars explain the likeness by the two peoples living as neighbours for a long age. Either way, when the legions came, the Britons met a language that was already family. They took hundreds more words from it, pont, ffenestr, llyfr, eglwys, ysgol, and kept their own."]
LATIN_CY = ["## Chwaer i’r Lladin",
   "Efallai fod y Gymraeg yn perthyn yn nes i’r Lladin nag i unrhyw un arall o ieithoedd mawr Ewrop. Mae llawer o ysgolheigion yn dal mai un iaith oedd iaith y Brythoniaid ac ieithoedd yr Eidal ar un adeg, cyn iddynt ymrannu: aeth un gangen yn Lladin, a’r llall yn Gymraeg. Yn ôl y farn hon, chwiorydd yw’r Gymraeg a’r Lladin.",
   "Yn y gramadeg y mae’r dystiolaeth, lle mae ieithoedd yn newid arafaf. Mae’r Lladin yn ffurfio’r radd eithaf ag -issimus; mae’r Gymraeg yn gwneud hynny ag -af, -haf gynt, a daw’r ddau o’r un hen derfyniad, -isamos. Mae gan y Lladin ffurf oddefol yn -r, amatur, ‘cerir’; felly’r Gymraeg, cerir. Roedd y ddwy unwaith yn dangos y genidol ar enw â’r un -i hir, ac yn llunio’r modd dibynnol yn yr un ffordd. Newidiodd y ddwy hefyd yr hen air am bump, penkwe, yn yr un modd anarferol yn kwenkwe, a roddodd quinque yn Lladin a pump yn Gymraeg.",
   "Yna dyna’r geiriau sy’n gefndryd o’u geni, nid yn fenthyciadau: tri a tres, cant a centum, môr a mare, tir a terra, rhi a rex, gwir a verus, hen a senex, enw a nomen, ci a canis, tarw a taurus. Mae rhai ysgolheigion yn esbonio’r tebygrwydd drwy i’r ddwy bobl fyw’n gymdogion am oes hir. Y naill ffordd neu’r llall, pan ddaeth y llengoedd, cyfarfu’r Brythoniaid ag iaith a oedd eisoes yn deulu. Cymerasant gannoedd yn rhagor o eiriau ohoni, pont, ffenestr, llyfr, eglwys, ysgol, a chadw eu hiaith eu hunain."]

