PEOPLE_LONG = {
 "iolo-morganwg": (
  ["Edward Williams was born in 1747 at Pennon in the Vale of Glamorgan and grew up at Flemingston, where he followed his father’s trade as a stonemason. He never went to school. His mother taught him to read, the poets of the Glamorgan hills taught him the strict metres, and he took the bardic name by which Wales knows him: Iolo Morganwg, Iolo of Glamorgan.",
   "He walked the length and breadth of Wales, and to London and back, searching farmhouses and mansions for old manuscripts and copying them by hand. A great deal of Welsh poetry and learning is known today because Iolo found it, copied it and kept it. With Owain Myfyr and William Owen Pughe he edited The Myvyrian Archaiology of Wales (1801–07), the first great printed collection of the nation’s early literature. His own papers, many thousands of pages, are among the treasures of the National Library of Wales.",
   "On 21 June 1792, on Primrose Hill in London, he held the first Gorsedd of the Bards of the Isle of Britain, inside a circle of stones, under the motto ‘Y Gwir yn erbyn y Byd’, the Truth against the World. In 1819, at the Ivy Bush in Carmarthen, he joined the Gorsedd to the eisteddfod. Every National Eisteddfod since has been proclaimed and crowned by his ceremony, under his sign of the three rays. He gave Wales its greatest national pageant.",
   "Iolo was a man of principle. He was a Unitarian who helped to found the denomination’s society in south Wales and wrote hundreds of hymns. He hated slavery: in his shop at Cowbridge he sold only sugar grown by free labour, and he would take nothing from his brothers’ estates in Jamaica. He stood for liberty, for working people and for the dignity of the Welsh language when few others did. Above all he believed that Wales was a nation with a great past and a right to institutions of its own: a library, an academy, a voice. The Wales of the Eisteddfod, the Gorsedd and the National Library is in large part the Wales that Iolo imagined."],
  ["Ganed Edward Williams yn 1747 ym Mhennon ym Mro Morgannwg a’i fagu yn Nhrefflemin, lle dilynodd grefft ei dad fel saer maen. Ni fu erioed mewn ysgol. Ei fam a’i dysgodd i ddarllen, beirdd blaenau Morgannwg a ddysgodd y mesurau caeth iddo, a chymerodd yr enw barddol y mae Cymru’n ei adnabod wrtho: Iolo Morganwg.",
   "Cerddodd ar hyd a lled Cymru, ac i Lundain ac yn ôl, yn chwilio ffermdai a phlastai am hen lawysgrifau a’u copïo â’i law ei hun. Mae llawer iawn o farddoniaeth a dysg Gymraeg yn hysbys heddiw am i Iolo ei darganfod, ei chopïo a’i chadw. Gydag Owain Myfyr a William Owen Pughe golygodd The Myvyrian Archaiology of Wales (1801–07), y casgliad mawr printiedig cyntaf o lenyddiaeth gynnar y genedl. Mae ei bapurau ei hun, filoedd lawer o dudalennau, ymhlith trysorau Llyfrgell Genedlaethol Cymru.",
   "Ar 21 Mehefin 1792, ar Fryn y Briallu yn Llundain, cynhaliodd Orsedd gyntaf Beirdd Ynys Prydain, o fewn cylch o feini, dan yr arwyddair ‘Y Gwir yn erbyn y Byd’. Yn 1819, yng ngwesty’r Ivy Bush yng Nghaerfyrddin, cysylltodd yr Orsedd â’r eisteddfod. Cyhoeddwyd pob Eisteddfod Genedlaethol byth er hynny, a choronwyd ei beirdd, drwy ei seremoni ef, dan ei Nod Cyfrin. Rhoddodd i Gymru ei phasiant cenedlaethol mwyaf.",
   "Gŵr o egwyddor oedd Iolo. Undodwr ydoedd a helpodd i sefydlu cymdeithas yr enwad yn y de, ac ysgrifennodd gannoedd o emynau. Casâi gaethwasiaeth: yn ei siop yn y Bont-faen ni werthai ond siwgr a dyfwyd gan lafur rhydd, ac ni chymerai ddim o ystadau ei frodyr yn Jamaica. Safodd dros ryddid, dros y gweithwyr a thros urddas y Gymraeg pan nad oedd llawer yn gwneud. Yn anad dim credai fod Cymru’n genedl ag iddi orffennol mawr a hawl i’w sefydliadau ei hun: llyfrgell, academi, llais. Cymru’r Eisteddfod, yr Orsedd a’r Llyfrgell Genedlaethol yw, i raddau helaeth, y Gymru a ddychmygodd Iolo."]),
 "owain-glyndwr": (
  ["Owain Glyndŵr was descended from the princes of Powys and of Deheubarth, studied law at the Inns of Court in London and proved himself as a soldier. On 16 September 1400, at Glyndyfrdwy, he was proclaimed Prince of Wales, and the whole country rose behind him.",
   "By 1404 he held almost all of Wales, took the castles of Harlech and Aberystwyth, was crowned, and called a parliament to Machynlleth. He wrote to the King of Scotland as a cousin, telling him that both were descended from Brutus, the first crowned king of Britain, the Scots through Albanactus and the Welsh through Camber. He wrote to the lords of Ireland, and he made a treaty of alliance with the King of France.",
   "In the Tripartite Indenture of 1405 he agreed a greater Wales reaching to the Severn, the Trent and the Mersey. In the Pennal Letter of 1406 he set out a free Wales with its own church under St Davids and two universities. Owain was never captured and never betrayed, and no one knows where he lies. Read the long read ‘The letters of Owain Glyndŵr’ for the full story."],
  ["Disgynnai Owain Glyndŵr o dywysogion Powys a Deheubarth, astudiodd y gyfraith yn Ysbytai’r Brawdlys yn Llundain a’i brofi ei hun yn filwr. Ar 16 Medi 1400, yng Nglyndyfrdwy, cyhoeddwyd ef yn Dywysog Cymru, a chododd y wlad gyfan y tu ôl iddo.",
   "Erbyn 1404 roedd bron y cyfan o Gymru yn ei ddwylo; cipiodd gestyll Harlech ac Aberystwyth, fe’i coronwyd, a galwodd senedd i Fachynlleth. Ysgrifennodd at Frenin yr Alban fel cefnder, gan ddweud wrtho fod y ddau’n disgyn o Brutus, brenin coronog cyntaf Prydain, yr Albanwyr drwy Albanactus a’r Cymry drwy Camber. Ysgrifennodd at arglwyddi Iwerddon, a gwnaeth gytundeb cynghrair â Brenin Ffrainc.",
   "Yn y Cytundeb Tridarn yn 1405 cytunodd ar Gymru fwy yn ymestyn at afonydd Hafren, Trent a Mersi. Yn Llythyr Pennal yn 1406 gosododd allan Gymru rydd â’i heglwys ei hun dan Dyddewi a dwy brifysgol. Ni ddaliwyd Owain erioed ac ni chafodd ei fradychu, ac ni ŵyr neb ble mae’n gorwedd. Darllenwch yr erthygl ‘Llythyrau Owain Glyndŵr’ am yr hanes llawn."]),
 "arthur": (
  ["Welsh tradition holds that Arthur was Athrwys ap Meurig, king of Gwent and Glywysing in the south-east of Wales. He was the son of King Meurig ap Tewdrig and the father of King Morgan, and he is named in the charters of the Book of Llandaff.",
   "The earliest writings about Arthur are all Welsh: Y Gododdin, the Historia Brittonum with its twelve victories, the Welsh Annals, the Black Book of Carmarthen and Culhwch ac Olwen. They set him among the saints and kings of Gwent and Glamorgan, with his court at Caerleon. Other historians may see it differently. Read the long read ‘Arthur, King of the Britons’ for the full story."],
  ["Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, Athrwys ap Meurig, brenin Gwent a Glywysing yn y de-ddwyrain, oedd Arthur. Mab ydoedd i’r Brenin Meurig ap Tewdrig a thad y Brenin Morgan, ac fe’i henwir yn siarteri Llyfr Llandaf.",
   "Cymreig yw’r holl ysgrifau cynharaf am Arthur: Y Gododdin, yr Historia Brittonum â’i ddeuddeg buddugoliaeth, yr Annales Cambriae, Llyfr Du Caerfyrddin a Culhwch ac Olwen. Maent yn ei osod ymhlith saint a brenhinoedd Gwent a Morgannwg, a’i lys yng Nghaerllion. Gall haneswyr eraill weld pethau’n wahanol. Darllenwch yr erthygl ‘Arthur, Brenin y Brythoniaid’ am yr hanes llawn."]),
}

